body:
**Shape.** A drop-on-overwrite of `a` on one arm while the borrow `b` of it is used only on the other arm.

```bezel
module Probe;

public sealed class R
  destructor
  end destructor;
  public method Use()
  end Use;
end R;

public class Program
  public static method Main(args: Array<string>): int
    var a := new R();
    var b := a;                       // a borrow of `a`
    if args.Count > 0 then
      a := new R();                   // drop-on-overwrite of `a`; `b` is NOT live here (its only use is on the other arm)
    else
      b.Use();
    end;
    return 0;
  end Main;
end Program;
```

**Measured on r35.** Compile error in both modes: `cannot consume \`a\` here: the borrow held by \`b\` is still live — its later use would read freed memory (consume-site freeze, §28.3/§28.7/§30.14 v0.180)`. There is no path on which `b` is used after the overwrite; the freeze is keyed on a flow-insensitive "used later in the source" rather than on liveness.

**Spec (v0.345).** K5 (§28.3.1): the freeze applies *"while a live local borrow of its current value exists"*; K5.5: *"liveness is the backward dataflow over one body"* — the standard backward liveness, on which `b` is dead at the `then` arm's assignment (its only use is on the `else` arm, which the `then` arm's path never reaches). Expected: accept (with the must-use warning on `a`'s scope-end drop).

Low severity (an over-rejection with an easy rewrite), recorded because the model's K5.5 reading and the compiler's disagree on a two-arm program and the campaign's freeze tests should pin which reading is meant.

**Differential numbers.** 1 of 6,000 sampled programs in the all-features `--if` sample (vars 3 / len 4) — the shape needs a borrow, an `if`, a consume on one arm and a use on the other, so it is rare at these bounds.

**Measured on r35** (`bezelc 0.0.0-f6863e62`, the 2026-10-27 release build; main may have moved since). Found by the model-vs-compiler differential (`scratch/bzdiff`, proposal 0047 §5.3 / soundness README §3.2): the executable ownership model (bzcore L1, spec v0.345) and `bezelc` disagree on this program; the model's verdict is the spec's sentence quoted above.
